<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Nyasaland</h1><p class="st-meta">1907 CE – 1964 CE · capital Zomba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate around Lake Malawi that resisted federation with the Rhodesias and became independent Malawi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1907 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-central-africa-protectorate">British Central Africa Protectorate</a>, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Harry Johnston</a> in 1891 after wars against Yao slave traders, was renamed the Nyasaland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>. It was given a governor, an executive council and a legislative council, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zomba">Zomba</a> remaining the capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nyasaland was a poor territory whose main export was labour: tens of thousands of men worked in the mines and farms of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-rhodesia">Southern Rhodesia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">South Africa</a>, while planters in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a> grew tobacco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> using thangata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenancy">tenancy</a>. In 1915 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptist</a> preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-chilembwe">John Chilembwe</a> led a short rising against planter abuses. Scottish missions made literacy unusually widespread, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyasaland-african-congress">Nyasaland African Congress</a> was founded in 1944.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Nyasaland was joined to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> in 1953 against strong African opposition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Kamuzu Banda</a> returned in 1958, a state of emergency followed in 1959, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi-congress-party">Malawi Congress Party</a> won the 1961 elections. Nyasaland became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> on 6 July 1964.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nyasaland.jpg" alt="Nyasaland" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Fry1989 eh? · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nyasaland_(1925%E2%80%931964).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1907 CE – 1964 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zomba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hastings Kamuzu Banda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chichewa, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nyasaland Protectorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=nyasaland" aria-label="Nyasaland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5644 2750 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nyasaland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5964 3006L5964 3006M5964 3006L4997 651M5964 3006L5842 3096"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5964 3006h0M4997 651h0M5842 3096h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5964 3006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5964 3006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zomba. <a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=nyasaland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
